    {
      "id": "_DSC9413",
      "file": "_DSC9413.jpg",
      "webp": "_DSC9413.webp",
      "thumb": "thumbs/_DSC9413.webp",
      "thumbJpg": "thumbs/_DSC9413.jpg",
      "width": 2821,
      "height": 4429,
      "aspect": 0.6369,
      "heightIn": 22,
      "widthIn": 14.01,
      "measured": true,
      "title": "Dhanna's Stone",
      "year": 2025,
      "medium": "Oil on canvas",
      "description": "Inspired by Bhagat Dhanna Ji, the farmer whose faith turned a stone into divine presence. In the film Bhagat Dhanna Jatt, the priest's ritual is empty, yet Dhanna's belief makes the stone sacred. His sincerity carries more weight than ceremony. The clip is from the movie Bhagat Dhanna Jatt(1974). I've been thinking about how far abstraction can stretch before we reach back for form. What would a shape mean if we couldn't see? What is the form of a feeling, or the outline of something invisible? We choose form because it comforts us, it helps us speak, to touch, to be seen. Rituals, forms and the shapes we create aren't truth on their own; they're subsets of presence, gestures reaching toward what's already here."
    },
